<?xml version="1.0"?>
<failureSpec>

    <!--
      Time-based, phase-driven fault specification for CppBedrock PBFT.

      Calibrated on CloudLab at 20,000 requests/s, four replicas on one
      32-core host, with the consensus window at 16 batches in flight. See
      CppBedrock/adaptive_timers.MD for the measurements.
      learning_agent/config/schedule_pbft.csv holds the best timeout per phase.

      Election timeout ladder: 250 (low), 550 (medium), 1200 (high).
      The medium rung was moved from 600 to 550; the numbers quoted in
      this comment were measured at 600 and have not been re-measured.

      Two mechanisms, kept separate.

      The network delay sets the healthy latency tail. Measured: p50 is 168 ms
      at the base 5 ms link, 335 ms at 5+50 and 630 ms at 5+150 - about 3 ms of
      tail per ms of one-way delay. A rung at or below its level's tail fires on
      ordinary latency and storms, with no fault injected at all. That is what
      removes 250 at the 5+75 phases, and both 250 and 550 at 5+175. Latency is
      near-deterministic - p95 runs about 20 ms above p50 at every level - so a
      rung only has to clear that spread to be removed by a tail.

      The delays were 50 and 150, where the removals held on 85 ms and 80 ms of
      margin. They were raised to 75 and 175 to put roughly 155 ms under each
      removed rung instead, since a margin of that size was being decided by
      jitter. The 5+75 and 5+175 tails are extrapolated from the fit above at
      about 405 ms and 705 ms; neither has been measured.

      The leader proposal delay D decides which of the surviving rungs fires.
      A rung below the delayed leader's latency escapes to a clean leader once
      per interval tick and keeps the healthy tail; a rung above it never
      reaches its deadline and pays the delay on every request instead.

      The delayed latency is not tail + D, and it is not linear in D either.
      Measured on the rung that sleeps through the delay, at base:

        D          120    150    200    400
        p50        293    321    513    721-860
        p95        318    349    685    -
        cost/D     1.04   1.02   1.73   1.38-1.73

      There is a knee between 150 and 200, and the p95 column says what it is:
      below the knee the delay only defers a proposal, while above it the held
      proposal occupies its consensus-window slot long enough to throttle the
      pipeline, so the cost grows faster than D and the spread widens with it.
      At 5+50 the cost is about 1.0*D throughout, because there the delay
      overlaps work already in flight. Not re-measured at 5+75.

      Each delay sits mid-window between the rung that must escape it and the
      rung that must sleep through it:

        phase           must escape     must sleep      window      D
        base + small    250             600             100-150    120
        base + big      600             1200            254-607    400
        5+75 + small    600             1200            265-865    500

      The small delay's upper bound is set by the transient, not by the median
      cost. pinLeaderWindow re-pins its victim once per interval tick and the
      pipeline drains and refloods around that move; the spike grows with D,
      and one crossing per tick is enough for a rung to escape, since the
      window will not re-pin afterwards. Measured against the 600 rung: at
      D=120 the spike never reaches it (0 view changes, delayed p50 293), at
      150 about once per two ticks (1, reproduced twice, p50 320), at 170 once
      per tick (2, and p50 reads back as the healthy 150 - it escaped), and at
      200 the steady state has degraded as well (p50 513, p95 685).

      Raising D brings the next rung up into play; it does not push the winner
      higher. So 5+175 carries no delay - its tail alone leaves only 1200.

      The 5+75 phase at t=900 carries no delay either, and so has nothing for
      550 to escape that 1200 sleeps through: 550 and 1200 both clear its tail
      and tie. schedule_pbft.csv labels it 550 by the prefer-the-lower-rung
      convention, but no arm is uniquely best there. Giving it a proposal delay
      in the 550/1200 window, as t=1200 has, would turn the tie into a phase
      that discriminates.

      netem jitter reorders packets and TCP treats reordering as loss, which
      at this rate costs more than half the throughput, so jitterRatio is 0.
    -->

    <schemaVersion>4</schemaVersion>
    <replicaIdType>global</replicaIdType>
    <warmUpTime>10</warmUpTime>

    <globalNetworkDelay>
        <delayMs>5</delayMs>
        <jitterRatio>0</jitterRatio>
    </globalNetworkDelay>

    <phases>
        <phase>
            <atTime>0</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>0</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>0</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>0</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <pbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <replicas/>
                </proposalDelay>
            </pbft>
        </phase>
        <phase>
            <atTime>300</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>0</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>0</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>0</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <pbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <interval>60</interval>
                    <replicas>
                        <replica>
                            <id>leader</id>
                            <delayMs>120</delayMs>
                        </replica>
                    </replicas>
                </proposalDelay>
            </pbft>
        </phase>
        <phase>
            <atTime>600</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>0</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>0</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>0</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <pbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <interval>60</interval>
                    <replicas>
                        <replica>
                            <id>leader</id>
                            <delayMs>400</delayMs>
                        </replica>
                    </replicas>
                </proposalDelay>
            </pbft>
        </phase>
        <phase>
            <atTime>900</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>75</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>10000</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>10000</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <pbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <replicas/>
                </proposalDelay>
            </pbft>
        </phase>
        <phase>
            <atTime>1200</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>75</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>10000</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>10000</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <pbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <interval>60</interval>
                    <replicas>
                        <replica>
                            <id>leader</id>
                            <delayMs>500</delayMs>
                        </replica>
                    </replicas>
                </proposalDelay>
            </pbft>
        </phase>
        <phase>
            <atTime>1500</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>175</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>10000</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>10000</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <pbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <replicas/>
                </proposalDelay>
            </pbft>
        </phase>
        <phase>
            <atTime>1800</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>0</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>0</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>0</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <pbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <replicas/>
                </proposalDelay>
            </pbft>
        </phase>
    </phases>
</failureSpec>
